OFL.txt is that LICENSE, unmodified, CRLF line endings included: the SIL Open Font
License 1.1 under "Copyright (c) 2019 - Present, Microsoft Corporation, with Reserved
Font Name Cascadia Code." The Reserved Font Name binds modified versions (OFL condition
3), so the built font's name may use neither Cascadia Code nor either of its words:
SIL's OFL-FAQ 1.1-update7, Q5.4, "You may not use individual words from the Reserved
Font Names".

The variable font's nameID 7 is "Cascadia Code is a trademark of the Microsoft group of
companies." The built files' nameID 7 carries it, after JetBrains Mono's own notice:
"Any trademark notices must remain in any derivative fonts" (OFL-FAQ 1.1-update7, Q3.7).

The variable font's own name table says something else. nameID 0 is "© 2021 Microsoft
Corporation. All Rights Reserved.", and nameID 13 opens "Microsoft supplied font. You may
use this font to create, display, and print content as permitted by the license terms
...". The built files carry JetBrains Mono's nameID 13 (the OFL sentence), and their
nameID 0 carries both projects' copyright lines. Which text governs was the licence
question (note AC), answered on 2026-11-03: the font is Microsoft's release under its
repository's LICENSE, the OFL 1.1 above; the repository carries it under the OFL, and
baud ships the built font under the OFL. Checked that day: the archive above,
fetched again from its URL, hashes to its sha256 above (GitHub's release API gives no
digest for the asset, so the hash is of that fetch); ttf/CascadiaMono.ttf in it is
byte for byte the CascadiaMono.ttf here (cmp); the tag v2407.24 is commit
56bcca3f2c1e4cb19458954f0e2bb4635960df91; and the licence fetched from the tag again
is byte for byte OFL.txt.

Designers, per the repository's README.md at the tag (line 78) and the font's nameID 9:
